From: MacGameFix
Subject: win32u: a shared-memory window surface is not flushed with the USER lock held

A window whose parent is in another process gets CodeWeavers' shared-memory surface
(CX HACK 23950). Its flush is a message to the parent's process, WM_WINE_FLUSHSHMSURFACE,
sent with send_message_timeout. No message is sent with the USER lock held:
send_inter_thread_message calls user_check_not_lock, which asserts.

apply_window_pos calls update_surface_region while it holds the window pointer, which is
the USER lock, and update_surface_region holds it too when it calls
window_surface_set_shape. That ends in window_surface_flush, and so in the surface's flush:
a driver call for every other surface, a message for this one. Whenever the surface has
something to flush at that moment, the process aborts:

    err:system:user_check_not_lock BUG: holding USER lock
    Assertion failed: (0), function user_check_not_lock, file sysparams.c, line 351.

Forza Horizon 5 (Steam) on NotProton's runner for CrossOver Preview 41303, 2026-11-26: the
game's WebView2 browser process (msedgewebview2.exe, thread CrBrowserMain) aborted so while
the Xbox sign-in page was up, and the game faulted two seconds later, as it does when its
WebView2 window goes away (a read of 0 at exe+0x53441F9). The return addresses of the
assertion's stack, read in CodeWeavers' win32u.so: apply_window_pos ->
window_surface_set_shape -> window_surface_flush (the call through funcs->flush) ->
send_message_timeout( 0x80000011, SMTO_ABORTIFHUNG | SMTO_BLOCK, 500 ) -> the assertion.
It was first seen on 2026-11-18, twice, while the sign-in page was typed in. A player who
is signed in already does not meet it: the browser's window is then never drawn.

With the lock held the flush now does nothing and says so, so the surface's bounds stay as
they are: the message loop's own flush (flush_window_surfaces), which holds no lock, sends
them, as it sends every other draw. The same code is in Wine 11.0 and 11.17 as CrossOver
ships them.

--- a/dlls/win32u/dce.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/dce.c
@@ -763,6 +763,12 @@
     struct flush_shm_surface_params params;
 
     TRACE("%p\n", surface);
+
+    /* The flush is a message to the parent's process, and no message is sent with the USER
+     * lock held: user_check_not_lock asserts. apply_window_pos and update_surface_region
+     * hold it when they set the surface's shape, which flushes. The bounds stay as they
+     * are, and the message loop's own flush, which holds no lock, sends them. */
+    if (user_lock_held()) return FALSE;
 
     params.section = HandleToLong( surface->section );
     params.hwnd = HandleToLong( surface->header.hwnd );
--- a/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
@@ -343,6 +343,11 @@
     pthread_mutex_unlock( &user_mutex );
 }
 
+BOOL user_lock_held(void)
+{
+    return user_lock_thread == GetCurrentThreadId();
+}
+
 void user_check_not_lock(void)
 {
     if (user_lock_thread == GetCurrentThreadId())
--- a/dlls/win32u/win32u_private.h
+++ b/dlls/win32u/win32u_private.h
@@ -206,6 +206,7 @@
 extern void user_lock(void);
 extern void user_unlock(void);
 extern void user_check_not_lock(void);
+extern BOOL user_lock_held(void);
 extern BOOL get_gpu_uuid_from_luid( const LUID *luid, GUID *uuid );
 extern BOOL get_gpu_info_from_uuid( const GUID *uuid, LUID *luid, UINT32 *node_mask, char *name );
 extern BOOL use_default_gamma_ramp(void);
